course: dictionary
lesson: 6778
title: Choosing with the word option
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An option is one of the things you can choose from. «The school trip has two options: the museum or the zoo.» Each option is a choice on the list, and you pick one.
explain: Option can also mean the freedom or right to choose. «You have no option but to wait» means you cannot choose anything else. To check, swap in choice: "no choice but to wait" still makes sense.
explain: A dilemma is a hard choice where every option costs something. «There is one place left on the school trip» is a dilemma, because picking one friend means leaving out another. Ask yourself: what is the best option here?
fact: An option is one of the things you can choose from.
fact: «Have no option but to» means you cannot choose anything else.
fact: «Keep your options open» means you have not decided yet, so you still have choices.
fact: In computing, options are settings you can change in a menu.
fact: A dilemma is a choice in which every option has a cost.
exercise: In a quiz, «Option B» means which choice? | *The second choice on the list | Bottom of the page | The answer you must write twice | The last question
exercise: «I have no option but to wait.» What does this mean? | Waiting is my favourite thing | *I am not able to choose anything else | I am waiting for a bus | I can choose any day I like
exercise: Which phrase means you have not decided yet? | Pay in full | Take no notice | *Keep your options open | Close the door
test: «Our teacher gave us four options for the project: a poster, a model, a song or a play.» How many options were given? | *Four | Ten | Two | Six
test: In maths, 3 shirts and 2 pairs of trousers give how many options? | 5 | 2 | *6 | 3
exam: Which of these is a dilemma, a choice where every option costs something? | Picking a film to watch on Friday | Deciding what colour to paint your room | *One lifeboat with room for only some people | Choosing between a red pen and a blue pen
